      "text": "We need to find the common values of the arithmetic progressions \\(\\{2, 5, 8, 11, \\ldots\\}\\) and \\(\\{3, 10, 17, 24, \\ldots\\}\\) and then determine the largest common value that is less than 500.\n\n**Step 1: Determine the general terms of the arithmetic progressions.**\n\nThe first arithmetic progression is \\(\\{2, 5, 8, 11, \\ldots\\}\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of this progression can be written as:\n\\[\na_n = 2 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 = 3n - 1\n\\]\n\nThe second arithmetic progression is \\(\\{3, 10, 17, 24, \\ldots\\}\\). The \\(m\\)-th term of this progression can be written as:\n\\[\nb_m = 3 + (m-1) \\cdot 7 = 7m - 4\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Set the terms equal to find common terms.**\n\nWe need to find \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) such that:\n\\[\n3n - 1 = 7m - 4\n\\]\nRearranging this equation, we get:\n\\[\n3n - 7m = -3\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Solve the Diophantine equation \\(3n - 7m = -3\\).**\n\nWe need to find integer solutions to this equation. First, we solve for \\(n\\) in terms of \\(m\\):\n\\[\n3n = 7m - 3 \\implies n = \\frac{7m - 3}{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\) to be an integer, \\(7m - 3\\) must be divisible by 3. We check the remainder when \\(7m - 3\\) is divided by 3:\n\\[\n7m - 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\implies 7m \\equiv 3 \\pmod{3} \\implies 7m \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nSince \\(7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\\), we have:\n\\[\nm \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\n\\]\nThus, \\(m\\) must be a multiple of 3. Let \\(m = 3k\\) for some integer \\(k\\). Substituting \\(m = 3k\\) into the equation for \\(n\\):\n\\[\nn = \\frac{7(3k) - 3}{3} = \\frac{21k - 3}{3} = 7k - 1\n\\]\n\nSo, the \\(n\\)-th term that appears in both progressions is:\n\\[\na_n = 3n - 1 = 3(7k - 1) - 1 = 21k - 3 - 1 = 21k - 4\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Find the largest common value less than 500.**\n\nWe need:\n\\[\n21k - 4 < 500\n\\]\nSolving for \\(k\\):\n\\[\n21k < 504 \\implies k < \\frac{504}{21} \\implies k < 24\n\\]\n\nThe largest integer \\(k\\) less than 24 is 23. Substituting \\(k = 23\\) into the expression for the common term:\n\\[\nn = 7k - 1 = 7 \\cdot 23 - 1 = 161 - 1 = 160\n\\]\nThus, the common term is:\n\\[\na_{160} = 21 \\cdot 23 - 4 = 483 - 4 = 479\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest value less than 500 that the arithmetic progressions have in common is \\(\\boxed{479}\\).",
