      "text": "To find the largest common value less than 500 in the arithmetic progressions \\(\\{2, 5, 8, 11, \\ldots\\}\\) and \\(\\{3, 10, 17, 24, \\ldots\\}\\), we start by expressing the general terms of each sequence.\n\nThe general term for the first sequence is:\n\\[ a_n = 2 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 = 3n - 1 \\]\n\nThe general term for the second sequence is:\n\\[ b_m = 3 + (m-1) \\cdot 7 = 7m - 4 \\]\n\nWe need to find values of \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) such that:\n\\[ 3n - 1 = 7m - 4 \\]\nSolving for \\(n\\) and \\(m\\), we get:\n\\[ 3n - 7m = -3 \\]\n\\[ 3n = 7m - 3 \\]\n\\[ n = \\frac{7m - 3}{3} \\]\nFor \\(n\\) to be an integer, \\(7m - 3\\) must be divisible by 3. Since \\(7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}\\), we have:\n\\[ 7m \\equiv m \\pmod{3} \\]\nSo,\n\\[ m - 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\\[ m \\equiv 3 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\\[ m \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3} \\]\nThus, \\(m\\) can be written as \\(m = 3k\\) for some integer \\(k\\). Substituting \\(m = 3k\\) into the equation for \\(n\\), we get:\n\\[ n = \\frac{7(3k) - 3}{3} = \\frac{21k - 3}{3} = 7k - 1 \\]\nSo, the general term that is common to both sequences is:\n\\[ a_n = 3(7k - 1) - 1 = 21k - 4 \\]\n\nWe need to find the largest \\(k\\) such that \\(21k - 4 < 500\\):\n\\[ 21k - 4 < 500 \\]\n\\[ 21k < 504 \\]\n\\[ k < \\frac{504}{21} \\]\n\\[ k < 24.3333 \\]\nThe largest integer \\(k\\) is 24. Substituting \\(k = 24\\) into the common term formula, we get:\n\\[ 21k - 4 = 21 \\cdot 24 - 4 = 504 - 4 = 500 \\]\n\nTherefore, the largest common value less than 500 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{500} \\]",
