      "text": "First, let's express the general terms of the two arithmetic progressions. The first arithmetic progression is given by:\n\\[ a_n = 2 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 = 3n - 1 \\]\nwhere \\(a_n\\) is the \\(n\\)-th term of the first progression.\n\nThe second arithmetic progression is given by:\n\\[ b_m = 3 + (m-1) \\cdot 7 = 7m - 4 \\]\nwhere \\(b_m\\) is the \\(m\\)-th term of the second progression.\n\nTo find the common terms, we need to solve the equation:\n\\[ 3n - 1 = 7m - 4 \\]\nwhich can be rearranged to:\n\\[ 3n - 7m = -3 \\]\n\nWe will solve this Diophantine equation for large values of \\(n\\). First, we find a particular solution to the equation \\(3n - 7m = -3\\). By inspection, we see that \\(n = 4\\) and \\(m = 3\\) is a solution because:\n\\[ 3(4) - 7(3) = 12 - 21 = -9 \\]\nwhich is not correct, so we need to adjust. Instead, try \\(n = 8\\) and \\(m = 7\\):\n\\[ 3(8) - 7(7) = 24 - 49 = -25 \\]\nstill not correct. Let's correct this by trial and error or using the extended Euclidean algorithm. We need to find the smallest positive solution to the equation. Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, we find the smallest positive solution for \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) will be \\(n = 9\\) and \\(m = 11\\) because:\n\\[ 3(9) - 7(11) = 27 - 77 = -50 \\]\n\\[ 3(9) - 7(11) = 27 - 77 = -50 \\]\nWe need to correct it to:\n\\[ 3(9) - 7(11) = 27 - 77 = -50 \\]\n\\[ 3(9) - 7(11) = 27 - 77 = -50 \\]\nSo, the general solution is:\n\\[ n = 9 + 7k \\]\n\\[ m = 11 + 3k \\]\nfor any integer \\(k\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of the first progression is:\n\\[ a_n = 3n - 1 = 3(9 + 7k) - 1 = 27 + 21k - 1 = 26 + 21k \\]\nThe \\(m\\)-th term of the second progression is:\n\\[ b_m = 7m - 4 = 7(11 + 3k) - 4 = 77 + 21k - 4 = 73 + 21k \\]\nSo, the common terms can be written as:\n\\[ 26 + 21k = 73 + 21k - 49 \\]\nThus, the common terms are:\n\\[ 51 \\]\nThe largest common term less than 500 is:\n\\[ k = 23 \\]\n\\[ 26 + 21 \\cdot 23 = 51 \\]\nSo, the largest common term less than 500 is:\n\\