      "text": "To find the common values of the arithmetic progressions \\(\\{2, 5, 8, 11, \\ldots\\}\\) and \\(\\{3, 10, 17, 24, \\ldots\\}\\), we first need to express the general terms of these sequences.\n\nThe first sequence is \\(2, 5, 8, 11, \\ldots\\), which has a common difference of 3. The \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence can be written as:\n\\[a_n = 2 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 = 3n - 1.\\]\n\nThe second sequence is \\(3, 10, 17, 24, \\ldots\\), which has a common difference of 7. The \\(m\\)-th term of this sequence can be written as:\n\\[b_m = 3 + (m-1) \\cdot 7 = 7m - 4.\\]\n\nTo find the common terms, we set the expressions for \\(a_n\\) and \\(b_m\\) equal to each other:\n\\[3n - 1 = 7m - 4.\\]\nRearranging the equation, we get:\n\\[3n - 7m = -3.\\]\nWe need to find the positive integer solutions to this Diophantine equation. We can rewrite it as:\n\\[3n = 7m - 3,\\]\n\\[3n + 3 = 7m,\\]\n\\[3(n + 1) = 7m.\\]\nThis means that \\(7m\\) must be divisible by 3. Since 7 and 3 are coprime, \\(m\\) must be divisible by 3. Let \\(m = 3k\\) for some integer \\(k\\). Substituting \\(m = 3k\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[3(n + 1) = 7 \\cdot 3k,\\]\n\\[n + 1 = 7k,\\]\n\\[n = 7k - 1.\\]\nSo, the \\(n\\)-th term that is common to both sequences is:\n\\[a_n = 3n - 1 = 3(7k - 1) - 1 = 21k - 4.\\]\nWe need to find the largest common value less than 500. So, we set up the inequality:\n\\[21k - 4 < 500,\\]\n\\[21k < 504,\\]\n\\[k < \\frac{504}{21} = 24.\\]\nThe largest integer \\(k\\) that satisfies this inequality is \\(k = 23\\). Substituting \\(k = 23\\) into the formula for the common terms, we get:\n\\[21k - 4 = 21 \\cdot 23 - 4 = 483 - 4 = 479.\\]\nThus, the largest common value less than 500 is \\(\\boxed{479}\\).",
